 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The haptic control loop performs tri-linear interpolation and vector operations. The virtual plane update (every $n$ loops) adds gradient computation (central differences). The cost is significantly lower than methods requiring multiple gradient searches per loop (like Blezek and Robb) but higher than simple density-based methods (like Avila and Sobierajski) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The haptic control loop performs tri-linear interpolation and vector operations [S1]. The virtual plane update (every $n$ loops) adds gradient computation (central differences) [S1]. The cost is significantly lower than methods requiring multiple gradient searches per loop (like Blezek and Robb) but higher than simple density-based methods (like Avila and Sobierajski) [S1].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal additional memory beyond the volumetric data and a few state variables (VHIP, Plane, Counter) [S1].
 *   **Measured Performance:** On an SGI Octane workstation with a 128x128x64 lung dataset, the method achieved an average servo time of 46.80 microseconds (21.37 kHz) [S1]. This is slower than Avila and Sobierajski (21.65 kHz) but faster than Blezek and Robb (17.36 kHz) [S1].
